@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "String Functions";
}

<div class="container-fluid py-4">

    <!-- =========================
         PAGE HEADER
    ========================== -->

    <div class="mb-5">

        <h1 class="fw-bold">
            SQL Server String Functions
        </h1>

        <p class="lead text-muted">
            String Functions in SQL Server are used to work with,
            manipulate, search, format and transform text or
            character data.
        </p>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         INTRODUCTION
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            What are String Functions?
        </h2>

        <p>
            String functions are built-in SQL Server functions that
            perform operations on character and text values.
        </p>

        <p>
            They are commonly used when we need to change the case of
            text, find the length of a string, extract a part of a
            string, replace text, combine strings or search for a
            particular character or word.
        </p>

        <div class="alert alert-info mt-4">

            <strong>Example:</strong>

            <p class="mb-0 mt-2">
                If a database contains a student's name such as
                <strong>"Rahul Sharma"</strong>, String Functions can
                be used to find its length, extract "Rahul", convert
                it to uppercase, or replace part of the name.
            </p>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         COMMON STRING FUNCTIONS
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Common String Functions
        </h2>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped align-middle">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>Function</th>
                        <th>Purpose</th>
                        <th>Example</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>LEN()</strong></td>

                        <td>
                            Returns the number of characters in a string.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            <code>LEN('Hello')</code>
                        </td>
                    </tr>


                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>UPPER()</strong></td>

                        <td>
                            Converts text into uppercase.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            <code>UPPER('hello')</code>
                        </td>
                    </tr>


                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>LOWER()</strong></td>

                        <td>
                            Converts text into lowercase.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            <code>LOWER('HELLO')</code>
                        </td>
                    </tr>


                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>LTRIM()</strong></td>

                        <td>
                            Removes leading spaces from a string.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            <code>LTRIM(' Hello')</code>
                        </td>
                    </tr>


                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>RTRIM()</strong></td>

                        <td>
                            Removes trailing spaces from a string.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            <code>RTRIM('Hello ')</code>
                        </td>
                    </tr>


                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>LEFT()</strong></td>

                        <td>
                            Returns a specified number of characters
                            from the left side.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            <code>LEFT('Hello', 2)</code>
                        </td>
                    </tr>


                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>RIGHT()</strong></td>

                        <td>
                            Returns a specified number of characters
                            from the right side.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            <code>RIGHT('Hello', 2)</code>
                        </td>
                    </tr>


                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>SUBSTRING()</strong></td>

                        <td>
                            Extracts a part of a string.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            <code>SUBSTRING('Hello World', 1, 5)</code>
                        </td>
                    </tr>


                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>REPLACE()</strong></td>

                        <td>
                            Replaces one part of a string with another.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            <code>REPLACE('Hello SQL', 'SQL', 'World')</code>
                        </td>
                    </tr>


                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>CHARINDEX()</strong></td>

                        <td>
                            Finds the starting position of a substring.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            <code>CHARINDEX('SQL', 'Learn SQL')</code>
                        </td>
                    </tr>


                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>CONCAT()</strong></td>

                        <td>
                            Combines two or more strings.
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            <code>CONCAT('Hello', ' ', 'World')</code>
                        </td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         1. LEN()
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            1. LEN() Function
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>LEN()</strong> function returns the number of
            characters in a string.
        </p>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Syntax
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>LEN(string)</code></pre>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Example
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT LEN('CIIT');</code></pre>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Output
        </h5>

        <div class="alert alert-success">
            <strong>Output:</strong> 4
        </div>

        <p>
            The word <strong>CIIT</strong> contains four characters,
            so LEN() returns 4.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         2. UPPER()
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            2. UPPER() Function
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>UPPER()</strong> function converts all
            alphabetic characters in a string to uppercase.
        </p>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Syntax
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>UPPER(string)</code></pre>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Example
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT UPPER('ciit training institute');</code></pre>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Output
        </h5>

        <div class="alert alert-success">
            <strong>Output:</strong> CIIT TRAINING INSTITUTE
        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         3. LOWER()
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            3. LOWER() Function
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>LOWER()</strong> function converts alphabetic
            characters in a string to lowercase.
        </p>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Syntax
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>LOWER(string)</code></pre>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Example
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT LOWER('CIIT TRAINING INSTITUTE');</code></pre>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Output
        </h5>

        <div class="alert alert-success">
            <strong>Output:</strong> ciit training institute
        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         4. LTRIM()
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            4. LTRIM() Function
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>LTRIM()</strong> function removes spaces from
            the beginning of a string.
        </p>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Syntax
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>LTRIM(string)</code></pre>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Example
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT LTRIM('   SQL Server');</code></pre>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Result
        </h5>

        <div class="alert alert-success">
            <strong>Output:</strong> SQL Server
        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         5. RTRIM()
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            5. RTRIM() Function
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>RTRIM()</strong> function removes spaces from
            the end of a string.
        </p>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Syntax
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>RTRIM(string)</code></pre>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Example
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT RTRIM('SQL Server   ');</code></pre>

        <div class="alert alert-success mt-3">
            <strong>Output:</strong> SQL Server
        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         6. LEFT()
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            6. LEFT() Function
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>LEFT()</strong> function returns a specified
            number of characters from the left side of a string.
        </p>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Syntax
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>LEFT(string, number_of_characters)</code></pre>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Example
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT LEFT('SQL Server', 3);</code></pre>

        <div class="alert alert-success mt-3">
            <strong>Output:</strong> SQL
        </div>

        <p>
            Here, 3 characters are returned from the left side.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         7. RIGHT()
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            7. RIGHT() Function
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>RIGHT()</strong> function returns a specified
            number of characters from the right side of a string.
        </p>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Syntax
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>RIGHT(string, number_of_characters)</code></pre>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Example
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT RIGHT('SQL Server', 6);</code></pre>

        <div class="alert alert-success mt-3">
            <strong>Output:</strong> Server
        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         8. SUBSTRING()
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            8. SUBSTRING() Function
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>SUBSTRING()</strong> function extracts a
            specified number of characters from a string.
        </p>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Syntax
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SUBSTRING(string, start_position, length)</code></pre>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Example
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT SUBSTRING('SQL Server', 1, 3);</code></pre>

        <div class="alert alert-success mt-3">
            <strong>Output:</strong> SQL
        </div>

        <p>
            The starting position is 1 and three characters are
            returned.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         9. REPLACE()
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            9. REPLACE() Function
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>REPLACE()</strong> function replaces all
            occurrences of a specified string with another string.
        </p>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Syntax
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>REPLACE(string, old_string, new_string)</code></pre>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Example
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT REPLACE('Welcome to Java', 'Java', 'SQL Server');</code></pre>

        <div class="alert alert-success mt-3">
            <strong>Output:</strong> Welcome to SQL Server
        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         10. CHARINDEX()
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            10. CHARINDEX() Function
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>CHARINDEX()</strong> function searches for
            a substring inside another string and returns its
            starting position.
        </p>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Syntax
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>CHARINDEX(search_string, expression)</code></pre>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Example
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT CHARINDEX('SQL', 'Learn SQL Server');</code></pre>

        <div class="alert alert-success mt-3">
            <strong>Output:</strong> 7
        </div>

        <p>
            The word SQL starts at position 7 in
            <strong>Learn SQL Server</strong>.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         11. CONCAT()
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            11. CONCAT() Function
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>CONCAT()</strong> function combines two or
            more values into a single string.
        </p>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Syntax
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>CONCAT(value1, value2, value3, ...)</code></pre>

        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Example
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT CONCAT('CIIT', ' ', 'Training Institute');</code></pre>

        <div class="alert alert-success mt-3">
            <strong>Output:</strong> CIIT Training Institute
        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         PRACTICAL TABLE EXAMPLE
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Practical Example with a Table
        </h2>

        <p>
            Suppose we have a Students table:
        </p>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE TABLE Students
(
    StudentId INT,
    StudentName VARCHAR(100)
);</code></pre>


        <p class="mt-4">
            Insert some sample records:
        </p>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>INSERT INTO Students
VALUES
(101, 'Rahul Sharma'),
(102, 'Priya Patil'),
(103, 'Amit Kumar');</code></pre>


        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Convert Student Names to Uppercase
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT
    StudentId,
    UPPER(StudentName) AS StudentName
FROM Students;</code></pre>


        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Find Name Length
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT
    StudentName,
    LEN(StudentName) AS NameLength
FROM Students;</code></pre>


        <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
            Extract First Five Characters
        </h5>

        <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT
    StudentName,
    LEFT(StudentName, 5) AS FirstFiveCharacters
FROM Students;</code></pre>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         WHEN TO USE STRING FUNCTIONS
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            When Do We Use String Functions?
        </h2>

        <ul>

            <li class="mb-2">
                When we need to change text into uppercase or lowercase.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                When we need to find the length of text.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                When we need to extract a part of a string.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                When we need to replace specific text.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                When we need to search for a word or character.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                When we need to combine multiple text values.
            </li>

            <li>
                When we need to clean or format text data.
            </li>

        </ul>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         IMPORTANT POINTS
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <div class="alert alert-warning">

            <h4 class="fw-bold">
                Important Points
            </h4>

            <ul class="mb-0">

                <li class="mb-2">
                    String functions are mainly used with character
                    and text data.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    LEN() is used to find string length.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    UPPER() and LOWER() change the case of text.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    LEFT() and RIGHT() extract characters from
                    either side of a string.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    SUBSTRING() extracts characters from a specified
                    position.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    REPLACE() replaces specified text.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    CHARINDEX() searches for a substring.
                </li>

                <li>
                    CONCAT() combines multiple values into one string.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         SUMMARY
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <div class="alert alert-primary">

            <h2 class="fw-bold">
                Summary
            </h2>

            <p class="mb-0">
                SQL Server String Functions are used to manipulate
                and process text data. In this topic, we learned
                commonly used functions such as LEN(), UPPER(),
                LOWER(), LTRIM(), RTRIM(), LEFT(), RIGHT(),
                SUBSTRING(), REPLACE(), CHARINDEX() and CONCAT().
                These functions are very useful when working with
                names, addresses, descriptions, codes and other
                character-based data.
            </p>

        </div>

    </section>


